   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to building condition surveys;\nand to repeal sections 409-d and 409-e of such law relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S4462",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4462-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§3641, 3602 & 409-d, rpld §§409-d & 409-e, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4462\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nbuilding condition surveys; and to repeal sections 409-d and 409-e of\nsuch law relating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to create efficiencies in conducting and\nsubmitting building condition surveys.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends paragraph c of subdivision 4 of section 3641 of the\neducation law related to the requirements for building condition surveys\nby establishing a staggered schedule and requiring visual inspections in\ncertain cases.\n\nSection 2 'amends subdivision 6-e of section 3602 of the education law\nby removing lead testing as a reimbursable building condition survey\nexpense under building aid.\n\nSection 3 amends subdivision 6-h of section 3602 of the education law by\nadding the cost of lead testing to building aid.\n\nSection 4 amends section 3602 of the education law by adding a new\nsubdivision 6-i, which adds the cost of visual inspections to building\naid for inspections done prior to 2023.\n\nSection 5 amends paragraph (a) of subdivision 2 of section 409-e of the\neducation law by changing \"annual visual inspection\" to \"periodic\ninspections\".\n\nSection 6 amends subdivision 1 of section 409-d of the education law in\nrelation to the provisions regarding \"periodic inspections\".\n\nSection 7 repeals section 409-d of the education law. Section 8 repeals\nsection 409-e of the education law. Section 9 sets the effective dates.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently every school district is required to conduct and submit to the\nNew York State Education Department a building condition survey every\nfive years on the same cycle. As a result, all 700 school districts seek\nengineers and architects to conduct the building condition survey at the\nsame time. Every school district then submits the surveys to NYSED at\nthe same time. And, as a result, school districts tend to submit new\nconstruction projects to NYSED for review and approval all at the same\ntime. This is an inefficient process that causes delays in the review\nand approval of construction projects and ultimately increase.\n\nThe bill language requires NYSED to establish a staggered schedule\nstarting in 2019-20. School districts who are delayed in submitting a\nbuilding condition survey will be required to conduct and submit a visu-\nal inspections that will be newly building aidable. This will ensure the\nsafety of building as the staggering cycle is completed\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nPotential short term costs associated with reimbursement of visual\ninspections. Projected long term saving as a result of more timely\nconstruction projects.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately upon its enactment, provided that\nthe provisions of section five and section six of this act shall take\neffect on July 1, 2019, and provided that the provisions of section\nseven and eight of this act shall take effect July 1, 2023; and provided\nfurther that the promulgation of any rule or regulation necessary for\nits implementation shall be authorized to be made on or before such\neffective date.\n",
